To type component props in TypeScript, you can use either an `interface` or a `type` to describe the component's props object. Here's how:

**Basic Approach:**
Create an interface or type that describes all the props your component accepts:

```tsx
interface MyButtonProps {
  /** The text to display inside the button */
  title: string;
  /** Whether the button can be interacted with */
  disabled?: boolean;
}
```

Then use this type when defining your component.

**Key Points:**
- The props should be described as an **object type** using either `interface` or `type`
- You can include JSDoc comments (like `/** ... */`) to provide inline documentation that will appear in editors
- You can make props optional by using the `?` modifier

**For More Complex Cases:**
- Use **Union Types** to describe props that can be one of several different types
- Use the **Creating Types from Types** patterns for more advanced scenarios

**Special Case - Style Props:**
When typing the `style` prop specifically, you can use `React.CSSProperties`:

```tsx
interface MyComponentProps {
  style: React.CSSProperties;
}
```

This provides auto-complete and ensures you're only passing valid CSS properties.

